  "body": "Stacked on #501 (which is stacked on #477) — merge those first. Until then this PR's diff against `main` also shows their commits; this PR's own change is commit acbc6b6c.\n\n## What changed\n\n`sinumerik.cps` lifted Z with `G53 Z0` in two places: the after-operation home move and the program end. That assumes machine Z0 is the top of Z travel. Siemens' public documentation leaves the reference point and machine zero to the machine builder, so the post now reads #495's `machine.safeRetractZ`, the same way #501 does for the Tormach post:\n\n- **Stated value** (0 included) posts `G0 G53 Z<v>` + `G0 G53 X0 Y0` for a home move, and `G53 Z<v>` at the end. The value is in mm, which is the unit this dialect writes. A non-zero value also adds the header comment `; SAFE RETRACT G53 Z<v> - STATED BY THE MACHINE`.\n- **No machine** (null or unnamed) keeps today's `G53 Z0`, byte-identical.\n- **A named machine that states no value** gets one counted `message()` naming the machine and the `safe_retract` key, and no retract is written. The XY half of the home move is dropped too, so XY never moves without the Z lift first. The in-app gate blocks the write.\n- **No name exemption.** #501 exempts Tormach because PathPilot defines the top-of-travel convention. Siemens documents no such rule, so a model named \"Siemens …\" is refused like any other.\n\nDocs: `assets/posts/AGENTS.md`, `assets/machines/AGENTS.md`, and the decision file `docs/decisions/503-sinumerik-safe-retract.md`.\n\n**Output change:** none for anything shipped. Every shipped mill file and the default `3axis-vmc.mch` (the conformance goldens' machine) state 0, and the goldens are byte-exact. A user machine without the key, `millturn.mch`, or a document saved before #495 with a mounted machine is now refused by name through this post.\n\n## How tested\n\nNew suite `crates/app/tests/sinumerik_safe_retract.rs`:\n- `a_stated_z0_and_no_machine_post_byte_identical`: null machine and default VMC keep `G53 Z0`. Every shipped Z0 machine posts the same program as the no-machine path, apart from the name line.\n- `a_stated_safe_z_posts_that_z`: covers both the mirror and the raw-file door. Z290 is posted at the home move and at the end, with the header comment. An inch file's 11.5 is posted as Z292.1.\n- `a_machine_without_a_safe_z_is_refused_by_name`: covers both doors, and a \"Siemens\"-named model as well. The machine is refused once and no G53 is written.\n\nTwo fixture updates: `coolant.rs` and `coolant_followups.rs` built a bare `Machine` that they post through every mill post. They now state `safe_retract_z: Some(0.0)`.\n\nGate:\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-post`: all green.\n- Every `camcraft-app` debug test binary (172) is green, including conformance (goldens byte-exact), tormach_safe_retract, after_op, posting, coolant, and program_comments. The only one not run is the slow `center_cut_multiaxis` lane, which does not post.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --tests -- -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real machine and no Hub sim.\n- The `G75` fixed-point form (Siemens' documented tool-change position move) was considered and not adopted. See the decision file.\n- The UI machine sheet still does not show the key (same as #501).\n\nChangelog: Siemens 840D post retracts to the machine's stated safe Z (`safe_retract`, machine coordinates) and refuses a machine that states none, instead of assuming machine Z0 is the top of travel\n\nFixes #503\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
